## Findings

### 1. `sync_dir` leaves unreachable code on Windows (build/lint break, plus behavioral divergence)

```rust
#[cfg(windows)]
{
    let _ = path;
    return Ok(());
}
#[cfg(not(windows))]
File::open(path)?.sync_all()?;
Ok(())      // <-- unreachable when cfg(windows)
```

On a Windows build the function body reduces to `{ let _ = path; return Ok(()); } Ok(()) }`, so the trailing `Ok(())` is dead and rustc fires `unreachable_code`/`unreachable_expression`. Any build using `-D warnings` (or `#![deny(warnings)]` in the workspace lint config) fails on Windows only — i.e. exactly the platform this delta was supposed to fix. Additionally, `File` is now referenced only in the `cfg(not(windows))` arm; if `store.rs` has no other `File::` use outside that arm, the `use std::fs::File` import becomes an unused-import warning on Windows too (same deny-warnings exposure).

Fix by cfg-splitting the whole body rather than early-returning out of a `cfg`'d block, e.g.:

```rust
#[cfg(windows)]
pub(crate) fn sync_dir(_path: &Path) -> Result<()> { Ok(()) }   // FlushFileBuffers is unsupported on dir handles

#[cfg(not(windows))]
pub(crate) fn sync_dir(path: &Path) -> Result<()> { File::open(path)?.sync_all()?; Ok(()) }
```

Secondary note on the same change: the previous implementation propagated `ENOENT`/open failures on Windows; the no-op version now returns `Ok(())` for a path that does not exist, so a caller that (intentionally or accidentally) relied on `sync_dir` to surface a missing/renamed directory now silently succeeds on Windows while still erroring on Unix. If any caller uses `sync_dir` as an existence assertion after a rename, that assertion is now platform-conditional.

### 2. Bound-retry topology check still omits branch verification for the `worktree == "."` (root) case

The new guard is gated on `intent.worktree != "."`:

```rust
if intent.worktree != "." {
    let expected = expected_worktree_path(...)?;
    ... registered branch → path must equal expected ...
}
let target_store = Store::new(&target);
let record = target_store.load_record(request.issue)?;
if record.claim... != Some(&intent.claim_id)
    || record.phase != Bound
    || git::run(&target, &["rev-parse", "HEAD"])?.stdout != intent.base_revision
```

For the in-place (root) topology, the only revision assertion is `rev-parse HEAD == intent.base_revision`. Revision equality does **not** imply HEAD is on `intent.branch`:

- Immediately after `git branch <intent.branch>`, the pre-existing branch (e.g. `main`) and `intent.branch` point at the same commit, so a root worktree still checked out on `main` passes.
- A detached HEAD at `base_revision` also passes.
- A user/agent `git checkout main` between the crash and the retry passes.

In all three cases the retry proceeds to `update_manifest_state(&target_store, issue, PreparationState::Bound)`, recording the issue as bound while the working tree is not on the durable intent branch — the same class of topology drift the non-root guard was added to catch, just on the other side of the `if`.

Add a symmetric assertion for the root case, e.g. require `git symbolic-ref --short HEAD` (or `rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD`) in `target` to equal `intent.branch` (and treat detached HEAD as `ReconciliationRequired`), and additionally verify that `intent.branch`'s registered worktree in `git::worktrees(store.root())` is the root itself, so a branch that has since been moved into a linked worktree is also rejected.
